\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Block means preserve the hierarchical precision}
\date{2026-11-03}

\p{Let #{p\ge 2} and #{k\ge 1} be integers, and let the sites be the
residues modulo #{p^k}. For #{0\le j\le k}, let #{E_j} replace the
value at each site by the mean of the sites congruent to it modulo
#{p^{k-j}}. Each class has #{p^j} sites, so #{E_0} is the identity and
#{E_k} is the mean of every site. These are the averages of the
[nested blocks](fdip-0099). Set #{P_j=E_j-E_{j+1}} for #{j<k} and
#{P_k=E_k}. For nonzero real coefficients #{c_0,\ldots,c_k}, the
hierarchical precision #{Q=\sum_{j=0}^{k}c_jP_j} is invertible.}

\p{Let #{A} send a function #{\xi} on these sites to the residues modulo
#{p^{k-1}} by the class means
##{(A\xi)(r)=\frac{1}{p}\sum_{t=0}^{p-1}\xi(r+tp^{k-1}).}
The matrix #{(AQ^{-1}A^{T})^{-1}} equals the hierarchical precision
#{Q'} of depth #{k-1} with coefficients
##{c'_i=pc_{i+1}\qquad(0\le i\le k-1).}
If every #{c_j} is positive, #{Q} is the precision of a centered
[Gaussian law](fdip-0005). In the counting basis, the second-moment
matrix of #{A\xi} is #{(Q')^{-1}}.}

\p{Fix #{\lambda>0} and a positive mass #{m}, and take #{c_j=\lambda^j} for
#{j<k} together with #{c_k=m}. Replacing #{A\xi} by
#{Z=\sqrt{p\lambda}\,A\xi} divides the precision matrix #{Q'} by #{p\lambda}.
The resulting matrix is
hierarchical of depth #{k-1}, with coefficient #{\lambda^i} on each
projector below the top level and with top coefficient #{m/\lambda}. That
top coefficient grows when #{0<\lambda<1}, stays unchanged when #{\lambda=1}, and
shrinks when #{\lambda>1}. In the sense that a mass is relevant when it
grows toward coarser scales, relevance holds only for #{0<\lambda<1}. The same coefficient
relation repeats at every depth: the second-moment matrix of each
block mean is the inverse of the coarser hierarchical precision. These matrix relations do not by
themselves produce a Gaussian measure on functions on the #{p}-adic
integers.}

\proof{
\p{Each #{E_j} is symmetric, because its matrix is constant on the
pairs inside a class and zero across classes, and #{E_j^2=E_j},
because a class mean is unchanged by a second average. If #{i\ge j},
congruence modulo #{p^{k-i}} is coarser than congruence modulo
#{p^{k-j}}, so each coarser class is a union of finer classes. A
function constant on a coarser class is constant on each finer class
inside it, and therefore #{E_jE_i=E_i}. That identity does not use the
sizes of the classes. For the other order, every finer class has
#{p^j} sites, so the mean on a coarser class is the mean of the finer
means and #{E_iE_j=E_i}.}

\p{In particular #{E_{j+1}E_j=E_jE_{j+1}=E_{j+1}}. Then
#{P_jE_{j+1}=E_jE_{j+1}-E_{j+1}^2=E_{j+1}-E_{j+1}=0}. If #{i>j},
then #{i\ge j+1}.
The size-free identity gives #{E_{j+1}E_i=E_i}, so
#{E_if=E_{j+1}(E_if)} and the image of #{E_i} lies in the image of
#{E_{j+1}}. When #{i<k} the same left factor gives
#{E_{j+1}E_{i+1}=E_{i+1}}, and both summands of #{P_i} land in that
image. When #{i=k}, #{E_{j+1}E_k=E_k}. A vector in the image of
#{P_i} is therefore #{E_{j+1}} applied to something, and
#{P_jE_{j+1}=0} gives #{P_jP_i=0}. Both matrices are symmetric, so the
opposite product vanishes as well. For #{j<k} the square expands to
#{E_j-E_jE_{j+1}-E_{j+1}E_j+E_{j+1}^2}, which equals
#{E_j-E_{j+1}-E_{j+1}+E_{j+1}=P_j}. The case #{P_k=E_k} is symmetric
and idempotent because #{E_k} is. The sum of the projectors telescopes
to #{E_0}. Thus the #{P_j} are pairwise orthogonal projectors summing
to the identity, and #{Q} multiplies the image of #{P_j} by #{c_j}.
Its inverse multiplies that image by #{c_j^{-1}}. The functions carry
the [counting inner product](fdip-0002). Since #{P_j} is
symmetric and idempotent, #{\xi\cdot P_j\xi=|P_j\xi|^2}, and the quadratic
form is #{\xi\cdot Q\xi=\sum_j c_j|P_j\xi|^2}. If #{\xi\neq 0}, some
#{P_j\xi} is nonzero because the projectors sum to the identity. Whenever
every coefficient is positive, the form is therefore positive, and it
is the precision of a centered Gaussian. By the
[inverse-precision theorem](fdip-000B), that law has covariance
operator #{Q^{-1}}. In the counting basis, the second-moment matrix of
#{A\xi} is #{AQ^{-1}A^{T}}.}

\p{Write #{S} for the lift that makes a coarse function constant on
each class:
#{(Sg)(r+tp^{k-1})=g(r)}. Averaging a constant returns the constant,
so #{AS=I}. The counting pairing gives
#{(A^{T}g)\cdot \xi=g\cdot A\xi}, and the right-hand side expands to
##{\sum_r g(r)\frac{1}{p}\sum_{t=0}^{p-1}\xi(r+tp^{k-1}).}
The coefficient of #{\xi(r+tp^{k-1})} is #{g(r)/p}, so
#{A^{T}=S/p}. The class mean is
#{(E_1\xi)(r+tp^{k-1})=(A\xi)(r)}. Averaging those #{p} equal values
returns #{(A\xi)(r)}, hence #{AE_1=A} and #{AP_0=0}.}

\p{For #{j\ge 1}, the coarse average #{E'_{j-1}} is formed on the
residues modulo #{p^{k-1}} by classes congruent modulo #{p^{k-j}}.
Fix a site #{x=r+tp^{k-1}}. Since #{j\ge 1}, the step #{p^{k-1}} is
divisible by #{p^{k-j}}, so the fine class of #{x} modulo #{p^{k-j}}
consists of the sites #{r'+t'p^{k-1}} whose coarse residue satisfies
#{r'\equiv r} modulo #{p^{k-j}}. Each such #{r'} occurs for all #{p}
choices of #{t'}, and #{Sg} depends only on #{r'}. The fine mean is
therefore the coarse mean of #{g}, which is
#{(E'_{j-1}g)(r)=(SE'_{j-1}g)(x)}. Thus #{E_jS=SE'_{j-1}}. Applying
#{A} and using #{AS=I} gives #{AE_jS=E'_{j-1}}, and
#{AE_jA^{T}=E'_{j-1}/p}.}

\p{For #{1\le j\le k-1} this difference is
#{AP_jA^{T}=(E'_{j-1}-E'_j)/p}. The top term is
#{AE_kA^{T}=E'_{k-1}/p}. Because #{AP_0=0},
##{AQ^{-1}A^{T}=\sum_{j=1}^{k}c_j^{-1}AP_jA^{T}.}
Reindex #{i=j-1}. The right-hand side equals
##{\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}(pc_{i+1})^{-1}P'_i,}
where #{P'_i=E'_i-E'_{i+1}} for #{i<k-1} and #{P'_{k-1}=E'_{k-1}}.
The same orthogonal decomposition on the coarser residues says that
this sum is the inverse of the hierarchical precision #{Q'} with
coefficients #{c'_i=pc_{i+1}}. The matrix #{AQ^{-1}A^{T}} is therefore
invertible, and its inverse is #{Q'}. If the fine coefficients are
positive, the coarse coefficients are positive as well.}

\p{Now take #{c_j=\lambda^j} for #{j<k} and #{c_k=m}, with #{\lambda>0} and
#{m>0}. Then #{c'_i=p\lambda^{i+1}} for #{i<k-1}, while
#{c'_{k-1}=pm}. Dividing #{Q'} by #{p\lambda} sends #{c'_i} to #{\lambda^i} below
the top level and sends the top coefficient to #{m/\lambda}. If #{Z} denotes
#{\sqrt{p\lambda}\,A\xi}, the second-moment matrix of #{Z} is #{p\lambda} times the
second-moment matrix of #{A\xi}, so the second-moment matrix of
#{Z} is the inverse of #{Q'/(p\lambda)}.
The top coefficient #{m/\lambda} is larger than #{m} when #{0<\lambda<1}, equal
to #{m} when #{\lambda=1}, and smaller than #{m} when #{\lambda>1}.}

\p{Repeat the construction at every depth. If positive coefficients
#{c^{(k)}_0,\ldots,c^{(k)}_k} satisfy
#{c^{(k-1)}_i=pc^{(k)}_{i+1}} for each #{i}, the second-moment matrix
of the depth-#{k} block mean is the inverse of the depth-#{(k-1)}
hierarchical precision. The same matrix identity repeats at every
depth. These identities do not by themselves produce a Gaussian
measure on functions on the #{p}-adic integers.}
}
